Each tank has a dedicated hydraulic system, a pallet in the former forward lower cargo compartment, which is now given over entirely to the firefighting equipment. A pallet carries:

- an **AC electric motor** driving a hydraulic **pump**, powered from 115 V AC through a 35 A breaker on the tank's own galley bus (GALLEY BUS 1, 2 or 3);
- a **pressure switch** that starts the pump when pressure falls below its cut-in point and stops it at its cut-out point, in a 3,000 psi class system;
- an **accumulator**, a nitrogen-charged vessel that stores pressurized fluid. Its nitrogen precharge is 1,000 psi, checked on the preflight;
- a **relief valve** that caps the pressure if the pump keeps running;
- the tank's **emergency accumulator** and dump valve ([Emergency drop system](#emergency-system)).

The pallets are not connected to the aircraft's hydraulic systems 1, 2 and 3. A failure of the aircraft's hydraulics does not affect the drop system, and the doors draw nothing from the flight control systems.

**Why an accumulator.** Opening a gate takes a burst of fluid in less than half a second, far more than a small electric pump can deliver in that time. The accumulator supplies the burst and the pump recharges it afterwards, in a couple of minutes from empty. A charged accumulator also lets the gate close, and keeps the latches working, for some time after the pump stops. The pump does not run continuously: an idle pack loses a little pressure through internal leakage and the pressure switch runs the pump for a few seconds every few minutes to top it up. A pack whose accumulator has lost its nitrogen stores almost nothing, so the same leakage empties it in seconds and the pump starts and stops every few seconds.

**When the pump may run.** The motor's contactor is closed only if all of these hold:

1. the tank controller is powered (its System Pwr breaker, DROP SYS POWER ON and MASTER HYD PWR ON);
2. the tank's HYD PWR switch is ON;
3. the pressure switch is calling for the pump, or RUN PUMP is held;
4. and the motor has its own supply: the tank's galley bus live and its 35 A breaker in.

The criteria the loading system was certified to explain its design:

- an average fill rate of at least 500 US gal per minute through the 3-inch couplings;
- equal levels in all compartments within 30 seconds of the loading pump stopping, and no compartment overflowing before the approved volume is reached;
- no leakage between compartments, which is why each tank's fill shut-off valve is closed in flight. Left open, a full tank would drain through the manifold into the one being dropped;
- a positive fill gauge visible to the loading crew at the loading point.

A float that fails to close lets its tank fill past the approved load until liquid pours out of its vent. Hot loading, with the No. 2 engine running during a quick turn and fuel and retardant loaded at the same time, may be authorized.
